{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>To give them the days of the heavens upon the earth.</b> Scripture teaches you that the land of Israel is given to Israel forever — like the days of the heavens upon the earth, which are forever; and if they are exiled from it, they are destined to return into it, for no nation or tongue settles it and holds fast to it except Israel." "<b>And it is fitting</b> further to say that this is a promise for the time to come, that by the merit of the Torah they shall lengthen their days and live long lives of four hundred and five hundred years — this is what is said: ״כימי השמים על הארץ,״ for indeed five hundred (ת״ק) years is the measure from the heavens to the earth. And so Isaiah, of blessed memory, prophesied concerning the future and said (Isaiah 65:22): ״כימי העץ ימי עמי״ — like the days of the tree, the days of My people — like that time of the first man, who ate from the tree and whose days and life were long, so shall the days of My people be in the time to come. And this shall be when the Shekhinah, which is the final ה (heh) of the Name, returns; for in this world the heavenly court does not punish until twenty years, but in the time to come, when the heh returns, they will not punish until one hundred, which is five times twenty, as it is written (Isaiah 65:20): ״כי הנער בן מאה שנה ימות והחוטא בן מאה שנה יקולל.״ And since they extend the life of the wicked and do not punish until one hundred, which is five times twenty, there is no doubt that the righteous man, who is blessed and meritorious, shall live at the very least five times one hundred, which is five hundred (ת״ק). And this is what Isaiah, of blessed memory, said: ״כימי העץ ימי עמי״ — and this is the Tree of Life, concerning which the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: the Tree of Life is a journey of five hundred (ת״ק) years. And this is what Moses said here: ״כימי השמים על הארץ״ — like the measure of the heavens upon the earth; and this and that are one, except that the master alluded and the disciple explained." "<b>And there is</b> yet another interpretation: that Scripture alludes to the combined lifespans of the patriarchs, for they lived among them all five hundred (ת״ק) years — for Abraham lived one hundred and seventy-five (קע״ה) years, and Isaac one hundred and eighty (ק״פ) years, and Jacob one hundred and forty-seven (קמ״ז) years; behold, five hundred and two (תק״ב) years that the patriarchs lived. The remaining years correspond to heaven and earth, and five hundred (ת״ק) is the measure between heaven and earth. And so you will find the hidden name of the name Shaddai, which is five hundred (ת״ק); and when the world expanded and was stretched to this measure of five hundred, then He said to His world ״די״ (enough), and this is what is said: ״אשר נשבע ה׳ לאבותיכם,״ who lived five hundred (ת״ק) years — ״כימי השמים על הארץ,״ which are five hundred (ת״ק), for everything is by the merit of the patriarchs. And it is further possible to say: ״כימי השמים על הארץ״ — for these are the heavens and the earth that are in Genesis, and this is the time of the world's Jubilee; and understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {2 "The arithmetic as printed yields 502 (תק״ב), not 500; the comment apparently accounts for this with the phrase 'the remaining years correspond to heaven and earth,' though the logic is dense. Translated hyper-literally; the apparent numerical tension is left as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:47.712916270Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}